$DIR/main.p:20:11: the initializer of cache allocates, but a freestanding program has no allocator during package initialization
    A freestanding program, which every kernel is, has no allocator while its
    packages are initialized: it installs one in its entry function with
    alloc.SetDefault. Package initialization runs before that, so an allocation
    there would have nowhere to come from, and no console to report the failure
    on.
    The initializer of cache makes a map (main.p:20).
    Go's runtime sets up its heap before any package is initialized. A
    freestanding Plutonium program brings its own allocator, so there is none
    until its own code installs it, and no console to report the failure on if
    an initializer tried.
    Declare cache without an initializer, and set it in the program's entry
    function after it has called alloc.SetDefault:
        var cache map[string]int

        func kernelMain(...) {
            alloc.SetDefault(heap)
            cache = ...
        }
    (see "Package initialization" in the Plutonium spec, section 15.2)
    (run "pluto explain E2586" for a longer explanation)

